6 of 171 posts.

Parents describe children aged two to three saying they are scared, resisting showers after an injury, fearing darkness, avoiding physical challenges, panicking around familiar people or activities, or becoming sad about growing up. They ask whether the fear is a phase, reflects an experience or change at home, or needs more support. Attempts include reassurance, adjusted lighting, breathing practice, encouragement and gentle exposure without forcing participation.

- "Recently I held her in my arms and rocked her and sang rockabye baby to her when we were playing around in front of the mirror. She loved it and keeps asking me to sing rockabye baby now. But as soon as I start singing it she whines and says she's scared." [ar002-0097]
- "we've been dealing with him not wanting to shower since, and have been very scared of the shower head. he will freak out and shake whenever we detach the shower head to use it. he keeps telling us to put it back up. he will cry every time we shower and honestly its been exhausting." [ar067-0019]
- "My son (2 years old) used to be a sleeper. But for the last two weeks, bedtime has become a complete nightmare. As soon as I turn off the main light, he starts clinging to me, crying, and pointing at the corners saying "scared."" [ar045-0010]
- "but he just doesn't really get out of his comfort zone much. we got him one of those woom balance bikes for his bday, he is scared to go on it. we got him a scooter for his 2 year old bday, he does that a little but overall isn't going really fast or turning or really using it like i see other kids his age do." [ar069-0088]
- "2- Today I went over to my sister’s house to visit my nephew since it was his birthday. My daughter has been to her house many many times. When I opened the car door, I said “we are going to see auntie and cousin!” And she immediately starts screaming and trying to buckle herself back into her car seat." [ar061-0075]
- "How do I help my daughter’s anxiety surrounding this topic. Should we just stop mentioning it? Is your kid like this? Is this just a common anxiety and nothing to worry about really?" [ar033-0137]

**But:** One family reports shorter shower-related crying after practising breathing and encouraging the child's bravery. "But recently, its gotten better. He cries still everytime we tell its time for shower, and he cries little bit when first in the shower but we've been practicing breathing exercise and encourage him how brave he is overcoming his fear. So his cries gone a lot shorter. He only cries in the beginning of showers and will be fine towards the middle and at the end. So im seeing hope." [ar067-0025]

