### The parent asks how to help when sensations or changes in routine cause distress.

11 of 171 posts.

These accounts describe two- and three-year-olds distressed by sound, grooming, clothing, touch, mess, perceived wetness or departures from an expected routine. Other children seek strong body movement or input while showing good language and everyday milestones. Parents ask about resources, specialist evaluation or occupational therapy, how much to accommodate, and whether the child will grow out of it; the effects include refusing activities, overheating in preferred clothes and family outings feeling impossible.

- "Is this normal? List of things that made him freak out (I.e., cry hysterically until almost throwing up sometimes) in the past month: hair getting cut (it’s currently 1/2 way cut), wrong kind of pants (NEEDS to be baseball pants/tight), hair gets wet (in tub), sister touched toy he’s not playing with, not wanting to wear a sweatshirt outside on a 40 degree day, and my personal favorite, the city workers cutting ‘his’ concrete." [ar049-0032]
- "My just turned 3 year old is very specific about bed time. It’s a heatwave here at the moment yet so flat of refuses to wear anything that’s not a tshirt/long sleeve and full length trousers tucked into socks. The mere suggestion of shorts upsets her." [ar061-0038]
- "My daughter (2 years, 3 months) absolutely freaks out over any sort of loud noise. The lawnmower, vacuum, hair dryer, etc. Even if we’re in the house and my husband is mowing the lawn she loses her mind. I bought her the sound proof ear muffs but she refuses to wear them." [ar006-0108]
- "My 2.5 year old is doing well developmentally, however he does not like messy play. At daycare he will not participate in any “messy” sensory activities (slime, playdough, mud, painting, etc.). I see pictures from the daycare and he’s the only one who doesn’t participate." [ar005-0046]
- "We see her sensitivity manifest in shyness with new kids (she’ll cry if others get within 5 ft of her at the park but at daycare she plays and interacts with all her peers and some of the older kids), she doesn’t love her hands being dirty and is hesitant towards some messy play but not all, and she hates her hair being brushed, nails trimmed, etc." [ar062-0051]
- "Her current additional quirk is things being wet. She is convinced that anytime myself or my husband touch something of hers, we've made it wet and she throws a tanty. Sometimes it's her own hands that are clammy and make things wet, and sometimes she is just convinced something is wet when it absolutely is not." [ar026-0037]
- "he is incredible sensory seeking— he throws a lot, flaps his arms when excited, rocks on his legs when standing/cant stand still, will walk on his toes, and just always seems to be moving his body in some way." [ar050-0057]

**But:** Sensitivity can be selective rather than applying to every messy sensation: one child avoids crafts but tolerates messy food. "He’s oddly enough much better with messy food- he still asks for a napkin to clean himself but doesn’t avoid messy food what so ever." [ar005-0048]

