### The parent wonders whether a repeated habit, demand or intense interest is just a phase.

14 of 171 posts.

Children aged two to almost four repeatedly mouth objects or hands, handle ears, rock or bang their head while settling, demand echoed words or confirmation that they are a baby, or become intensely focused on particular toys, colours, characters or music. Other posts concern nonstop singing, suddenly needing one stuffed bear, or choosing music videos instead of shows. Parents ask how long it lasts, whether it suggests OCD or another issue, and how to respond when redirection fails or the habit becomes disruptive or unsafe. In the worsening bedtime-rocking account, the parent says lack of insurance has blocked access to a pediatrician.

- "He watches videos on vacuuming, pretends to vacuum, has vacuum toys, will point out vacuums anywhere he sees them" [ar007-0073]
- "I'm at my wit's end with my 28-month-old's duck obsession. She won't stop talking about ducks and constantly begs me to buy her one from Amazon. I've tried distracting her by saying we'll get one later, but she's too smart for that. Now she's demanding that we go to Target or the park to see ducks." [ar076-0165]
- "I have twin boy toddlers who are obsessed with System of a Down. The first time that they ever heard a song of theirs, they cried for me to play it again and again and again and again." [ar038-0077]
- "And if I don’t repeat the “key phrase”, you’d think a national tragedy just happened. She absolutely loses her shit. She’ll start by repeating it over and over, growing more and more frustrated, until I do repeat it or it turns into a breakdown." [ar056-0148]
- "She says this every 10 minutes or less some parts of the day and gets very upset when we don't respond that we understand that she is a tiny tiny eencie weencie tiny baby." [ar046-0109]
- "I have tried giving him a fidget toy to hold instead, redirecting, saying “hands down” or “hands out of mouth” and it works for a minute and then he’s back to putting his hands in his mouth." [ar041-0107]
- "My 2-year-old has been rocking himself to sleep for a while now, but lately it has gotten a lot worse. He rocks back and forth pretty hard when he’s trying to fall asleep, sometimes repeatedly hitting his head against the wall/headboard. I moved his bed away from the wall, but he’ll still try to rock into whatever is behind him." [ar004-0130]

**But:** Some parents explicitly limit the concern to one pattern rather than reporting wider developmental difficulty. "It’s really just this, besides things that I can guarantee are typical." [ar056-0153]

