### The parent asks whether disrupted sleep is a regression, a new skill or a need for more reassurance.

12 of 23 posts.

Parents of two- and three-year-olds describe losing independent sleep, prolonged bedtime requests or exits, night crying or screaming, and a nap that is disappearing or moving too late. They consider separation anxiety, teething, illness, growth, potty learning, language changes or sleep schedules, and want to know whether to wait or change their response. Attempts include lights, reassurance, staying nearby, bringing the child into bed, routine or nap changes, exercise and a GP visit with an antihistamine trial.

- "My now 2 year old has always been a great sleeper, slept through the night, puts herself to sleep, and self soothes if she woke up. All of a sudden about two weeks ago that has stopped. She can’t be rocked to sleep and put back in the crib or self soothe anymore. She needs mama and to sleep with mama which again use to never happen. Is this a normal sleep regression? First time mom here so I’m curious what to do 😩" [ar079-0001]
- "In the post-sickness sleeplessness phase, he has seemed to develop a fear of the dark too? We’ve added nightlights and seem to have that under control but he still is just constantly needing more support. He lays down and then within 5 minutes he’s yelling for a drink of water, needing a different stuffy, etc. This happens back and forth a few times and then he eventually gives in but it sometimes takes an hour of convincing him to sleep lol." [ar056-0002]
- "Anything from a sad whimper to a full on scream. And she doesn’t seem to be fully awake for this. I can go into her room, and soothe her by talking to her and patting her back and she will be fine. Out like a light. But it doesn’t matter how long I do this, as soon as I walk out of the room, she begins again." [ar059-0051]
- "Daughter (4 in two months) dropped the nap few months ago. Suddenly has stopped going to bed on time. Rocking 45 mins, trying to lay down and make her sleep was easy for few months and a relief. Is this a growth spurt? Have tried it all, wind down time, bath before bed experiment etc you name it. The other day she walked and bicycled a lot (I had like 15K steps) yet didn’t sleep until 11pm. And of course will still wake up between 6am-7:15am" [ar073-0049]
- "She's either really cranky because she doesn't get a nap or she struggles to fall asleep at night because she naps so close to bed time." [ar075-0186]
- "Every night we put him to bed, and he’ll chill for a bit but then get up to come see us. Can be once or sometimes up to 6+ times. Various reasons (I want socks, I want a hug, I can’t find X plushy, or just not wanting to sleep)." [ar034-0130]
- "My wife and I (f/f) take it in turns but we’ve hit our first parent preference stage so LO only wants me. Funnily enough, once I was away overnight she settled no problem! We’re pretty adamant we won’t take her into our bed, but I don’t think it would help anyway she’d be too excited." [ar038-0137]

**But:** Sleep or separation difficulty can be specific to a parent or setting, rather than occurring at nursery and at every nap as well. "She’s for sure been extra clingy with me in general! She’s also totally fine going to sleep for naps and at nursery. I will try this, thank you!" [ar038-0143]

