### The parent worries that the child's size or growth trajectory means something is wrong.

7 of 171 posts.

Parents compare height, weight and changing percentiles with siblings, parents or earlier measurements, asking whether a small child will catch up, a high-percentile toddler will lean out, or exceptional height needs investigation. The children include a nearly-six-month-old, a seventeen-month-old, and two- and three-year-olds. Some have already discussed growth with a doctor or dietitian, while others seek stories that would make the measurements less worrying.

- "She’s always been really bad for weight, but over all in the last year she’s gained less than 2lbs and less than an inch. […] At her two year appointment she was 98th for height & 35th for weight. This drop is so concerning to me and sending me into a spiral." [ar051-0075]
- "My daughter is 3yo and is 87cm (approx 34 inches) tall. I’m small (1,56cm / 5’2) and my partner is 1,75cm / 5’8. I understand this can be (probably is) genetics but I don’t why it worries me a lot that she’s been hovering around 1-2% height percentile for the past year. Sometimes I think if she was just above 5-10% I’d be much less worried.." [ar010-0085]
- "However my youngest… dropped to the 50th & has stayed there since about 1yr. he’s a bit harder to feed but I wouldn’t say he eats much less." [ar055-0016]
- "My son is 3 years and 3 months old and he's 3 foot 7." [ar024-0035]
- "I am now having a second baby who is a boy. I don’t know why but I feel like he is less chunky and smaller than my daughter. He is about to be 6 months next week and he weighs at 17.5 lbs and \~27 ish. Im worried he is going to be short like me. He just got diagnosed that he has CMPA but the doctor is not worried about his growth at all. So I’m currently like what my title says." [ar031-0054]
- "My second child (17m) was born on the 50th percentile of height. After starting solids, his height keeps falling down until now he’s on the 6th percentile 🫠" [ar063-0115]
- "Anyone ever had a high percentile (90s) 2 year old that ended up leaning out and becoming a very average kid later? Is BMI now indicative of later?" [ar049-0082]

**But:** The parents' concern does not always match a clinician's: the infant's doctor is explicitly described as unconcerned about growth. "the doctor is not worried about his growth at all." [ar031-0054]

