### The parent asks whether everyday movement and self-care should be easier by now.

7 of 171 posts.

Parents ask about falls, inward-turning feet, reluctance to walk on outings, dressing, sock removal or a child's insistence that mother perform ordinary tasks. Most children are two or three, including an almost-four-year-old whose dressing and night wetting prompt a learning-disability question. They describe demonstrations, encouragement, protective hats, carrying or using a stroller, and visits to an orthopaedic doctor, while comparing the specific difficulty with other skills the child manages.

- "It's gotten to the point where the skin in that spot is permanently damaged! Even when there wasn't a fall in a while, it's slightly discoloured and looks like scar tissue." [ar032-0008]
- "My preschooler is almost 4 and he struggles with getting dressed (ie looking at the writing and label for shirt and pants) and is still peeing his pants at night frequently. I’m trying to be patient but also worried there is some sort of learning disability. Can someone give me some insight?" [ar038-0019]
- "Our toddler is about to be 3 and she always hounds us to be carried or wants to sit in her stroller when we are out and about at the grocery store, mall, etc. She doesn’t do this when we are playing at the park." [ar008-0050]
- "Has anyone else had a similar situation and was there anything you did you help your child walk with straight feet?" [ar035-0131]
- "He doesn't want to do anything on his own - I have to push him to try to do things (I.e., pick up and eat his own sandwich, wipe his hands, take his own shoes off, etc.) It's usually, "Mama do it!"" [ar077-0142]
- "I have shown him how to hook his finger in the back of the sock to pull and then he will copy me and do it. But then the next time he does it he’s pulling at the front again and screaming." [ar055-0152]

**But:** One orthopaedic assessment found the hips and bones sound and recommended more time rather than braces or special shoes. "I took her back today because it hasn’t corrected itself but he mentioned that her hips looks good and that the bones are fine they just need a little more time to adjust and that he wouldn’t recommend braces or even special shoes." [ar035-0129]

