# baby-development: pattern 1, Is this normal, or is something wrong with my child? (children of 2 and 3 years)

**Written:** 2026-11-20 from baby-development/evidence/2026-11/age-reread/digest-02.md
**Reviewed by the owner:** not yet

## Limits

These posts come from a random, keyword-free sample of 20,000 posts in twelve parenting subreddits, 1 April–24 October 2026: 3,811 were on-niche, including 1,340 about children of two and three years. This pattern’s supplied digest contains 171 on-niche and 23 near posts; each count is posts tagged by one reader, not people or market prevalence. Forms from different digest readers are merged by judgement; here there is only one digest, whose forms and counts remain intact.

## On-niche posts

### The parent asks whether intense tantrums exceed the usual toddler stage.

15 of 171 posts.

Screaming, reversals and head-banging prompt evaluation questions; one parent reports repeated pinching and asks how to stop.

- "If I give them the "wrong" cup, cut their food differently than they expected, tell them it's time to leave the playground, or even help them with something they wanted to do themselves, it can turn into 20–30 minutes of crying, screaming, and refusing to calm down." [ar070-0068]
- "Não é a primeira vez que belisco ela." [ar018-0163]

**But:** Head-banging is not always frequent. "She’s done it twice now, once when she was 2, and now today." [ar029-0068]

### The parent wonders whether a repeated habit or intense interest will pass.

14 of 171 posts.

Mouthing, bedtime rocking, demanded responses and fixations range from disruptive to unsafe; insurance blocks one family’s pediatrician access.

- "I have twin boy toddlers who are obsessed with System of a Down. The first time that they ever heard a song of theirs, they cried for me to play it again and again and again and again." [ar038-0077]
- "I have tried giving him a fidget toy to hold instead, redirecting, saying “hands down” or “hands out of mouth” and it works for a minute and then he’s back to putting his hands in his mouth." [ar041-0107]

**But:** Concern can be confined to one habit. "It’s really just this, besides things that I can guarantee are typical." [ar056-0153]

### The parent asks whether a bodily change is developmental or needs care.

14 of 171 posts.

Vomiting, tiredness, teeth, eyes, staring, falls and a reported seizure prompt checks; supplied ages include sixteen and twenty-one months.

- "My 2.5 year old daughter eats until she throws up and I genuinely don’t know what to do anymore." [ar034-0025]
- "Does sucking his thumb only to help him fall asleep and at night will still negatively impact his teeth and palate development? Any positive or negative experience you can share?" [ar064-0039]

**But:** Distress need not be general. "I would describe him as a happy child overall, and our home situation is stable and positive." [ar072-0081]

### The parent worries about what the child’s emotional responses mean.

13 of 171 posts.

Concerns span people-pleasing, sensitivity, discipline, cry-singing, quiet reunions and apparent indifference to others’ suffering, including killing live minnows.

- "I don't know if I'm blowing this out of proportion because of my own childhood and this is developmentally normal, but it does not feel normal and I do not want my son to feel like he has to walk on eggshells/always please us when I feel like we don't even give off that expectation toward him." [ar066-0029]
- "I’m worried that my bf’s son seemed to show no regard for life or feeling in his conscious for killing like that. Most of all, I’m worried about potentially blending our families and I know that violence towards animals in childhood can be a sign of psychopathy or something." [ar062-0060]

**But:** A child wanting others sad also shows affection. "On the flip side, he is super affectionate with me now, always randomly saying “I love you” and asking to snuggle in bed. He also asks to see other kids he’s familiar with like his cousins." [ar020-0063]

### The parent cannot reconcile the child’s strengths with possible developmental differences.

12 of 171 posts.

Parents consider autism, ADHD or assessment; two supplied posts appear cross-posted, and one writer says AI arranged their own thoughts.

- "It seems like there is a huge discrepancy between the things he can and can't do. Some of it you would not expect untill he'd be 4-5 years old and other things you'd expect him to already know for over a year. None of the other kids had this. They all had their strengths and weaknesses, but it was usually balanced a bit. With my son, it seems to be all over the place." [ar048-0118]
- "Any thoughts? Any follow up questions? We fully intend to take him to his appointments and want to schedule an eval. I took the MCHAT-R and he scored a hard 3, with like 2 or 3 Maybes, but I've just been freaking out lately...." [ar025-0055]

**But:** Social strengths coexist. "Shows empathy (for example, patting someone when they’re hurt)." [ar007-0093]

### The parent worries when the child does not join in or speak to others.

12 of 171 posts.

Watching, solitary play, silence or changed sociability prompt comparisons and questions about shyness, adjustment or selective mutism.

- "I know every kid is different, but my son is no where near that level of pretend play. Is that normal developmental variation?" [ar056-0169]
- "When she's expected to interact or we gently prompt her, she says "I'm not ready" or "I'm uncomfy."" [ar028-0183]

**But:** Silence can coexist with enjoyment of school. "" [ar056-0092]

### The parent wants to know whether the child can communicate as expected.

11 of 171 posts.

Concerns range from single words to fluent but repetitive speech, few questions or difficulty recounting the past.

- "My daughter is 3.5 years old. She knows all the colors. She knows how to say specific things such as shoes, socks, pants, hat, pizza, water, ice cream, lollipop.. and a few other single words. But she doesn’t talk in sentences. She still speaks in gibberish baby talk." [ar075-0114]
- "He knows hundreds of words but uses them mostly to label or repeat. When he sees things he will say “it’s a ——“ for whatever it is (ex: it’s a cat!)." [ar008-0068]

**But:** Some language is advanced. "She asks for help, can name our entire massive family, expresses emotions etc." [ar036-0058]

### The parent asks how to handle distress over sensations or routine changes.

11 of 171 posts.

Sound, clothing, grooming, mess and unexpected changes restrict activities; some children seek strong movement instead.

- "My just turned 3 year old is very specific about bed time. It’s a heatwave here at the moment yet so flat of refuses to wear anything that’s not a tshirt/long sleeve and full length trousers tucked into socks. The mere suggestion of shorts upsets her." [ar061-0038]
- "My daughter (2 years, 3 months) absolutely freaks out over any sort of loud noise. The lawnmower, vacuum, hair dryer, etc. Even if we’re in the house and my husband is mowing the lawn she loses her mind. I bought her the sound proof ear muffs but she refuses to wear them." [ar006-0108]

**But:** Messy food can be tolerated despite craft avoidance. "He’s oddly enough much better with messy food- he still asks for a napkin to clean himself but doesn’t avoid messy food what so ever." [ar005-0048]

### The parent asks how much reassurance and closeness the child should need.

9 of 171 posts.

Contact demands, separation distress or unexpectedly no longer calling for parents raise questions about security and independence.

- "If he didn’t see me leave the room and realizes I’m gone, he will starting running around the house screaming and crying." [ar058-0045]
- "but my (almost) 4 year-old tells me almost daily that she doesn't like when I leave her and it CRUSHES me." [ar039-0152]

**But:** Separation can be easy elsewhere. "" [ar058-0046]

### The parent asks whether movement or inattention needs more investigation.

8 of 171 posts.

Activity, risky impulses or zoning out raise questions about temperament, screens, ADHD and supporting learning.

- "My point is that this age is an exhausting line between "Let them do it, they're learning" and "I want him to be entertained in a way that's not stressful for me or dangerous for him"." [ar015-0087]
- "I guess my biggest question is, how do I know if he is simply a very active and advanced 3-year-old, or if there is something else I should be paying attention to?" [ar007-0128]

**But:** Attention can improve with engagement. "He can’t really sit still unless he’s being actively engaged (like reading together or doing something hands-on)." [ar078-0004]

### The parent wants help stopping injury or damage before it worsens.

7 of 171 posts.

Biting, scratching, hitting and property damage bring injuries, preschool concerns and, in one account, an urge to hit that the parent has not acted on.

- "My arms are usually covered in scabs from him gouging me with his nails, and bruises from his bites." [ar033-0069]
- "My son is 3 and just started pre-k 2 weeks ago, up until now he’s been home. He is hitting all the kids, all the time. The teacher has said he can’t “safely participate” in any activity without 1 on 1 supervision." [ar081-0132]

**But:** Hair-pulling can be absent at nursery. "Il va 3 jours par semaine dans une micro-crèche, et il ne tire pas les cheveux et ne présente pas ce comportement." [ar081-0147]

### The parent questions another adult’s expectations of the child.

7 of 171 posts.

Teachers, relatives, sitters or doctors unsettle a previously reassuring view of ability or behaviour.

- "But she’s 2 and according to her doc at her yearly checkup, this is all emotion regulation and she’s not showing signs of autism and it’s far too early for adhd." [ar032-0016]
- "To be told it might need more investigation was a gut punch to say the least and now we’re questioning everything." [ar032-0099]

**But:** Parents sometimes see the same difficulties at home. "To be told it might need more investigation was a gut punch to say the least and now we’re questioning everything." [ar032-0099]

### The parent worries that growth measurements signal a problem.

7 of 171 posts.

Both smallness and exceptional size prompt comparison; this supplied form also includes a nearly-six-month-old and a seventeen-month-old.

- "My son is 3 years and 3 months old and he's 3 foot 7." [ar024-0035]
- "Anyone ever had a high percentile (90s) 2 year old that ended up leaning out and becoming a very average kid later? Is BMI now indicative of later?" [ar049-0082]

**But:** The infant’s doctor is unconcerned. "the doctor is not worried about his growth at all." [ar031-0054]

### The parent asks whether movement and self-care should be easier.

7 of 171 posts.

Falls, inward feet, dressing, socks or demands for help prompt comparison, demonstrations and medical consultation.

- "My preschooler is almost 4 and he struggles with getting dressed (ie looking at the writing and label for shirt and pants) and is still peeing his pants at night frequently. I’m trying to be patient but also worried there is some sort of learning disability. Can someone give me some insight?" [ar038-0019]
- "Our toddler is about to be 3 and she always hounds us to be carried or wants to sit in her stroller when we are out and about at the grocery store, mall, etc. She doesn’t do this when we are playing at the park." [ar008-0050]

**But:** One orthopaedic assessment recommends time. "" [ar035-0129]

### The parent wants to understand new fears and make the child feel safe.

6 of 171 posts.

Darkness, showers, familiar people, challenges or growing up become frightening; parents try reassurance and gentle exposure.

- "Recently I held her in my arms and rocked her and sang rockabye baby to her when we were playing around in front of the mirror. She loved it and keeps asking me to sing rockabye baby now. But as soon as I start singing it she whines and says she's scared." [ar002-0097]
- "My son (2 years old) used to be a sleeper. But for the last two weeks, bedtime has become a complete nightmare. As soon as I turn off the main light, he starts clinging to me, crying, and pointing at the corners saying "scared."" [ar045-0010]

**But:** One family reports improvement. "" [ar067-0025]

### The parent wants boundaries for body curiosity without shaming the child.

6 of 171 posts.

Touching, exposure, apparent self-stimulation, jokes, requests and drawings prompt normality and privacy questions; some fear inappropriate touching.

- "My almost 3-year-old has started doing a pose where she kind of lays on the floor and clenches her legs together and pushes forward on her other leg.  She says she's doing yoga, but I suspect it's self-stimulatory in some way." [ar020-0082]
- "One night while I was kissing her face/head, she said “kiss my \[vulva\],” then “kiss my butt,” and laughed. I told her we don’t kiss private parts and redirected her." [ar073-0130]

**But:** One parent reports easy redirection and no disclosure. "" [ar073-0131]

### The parent asks whether changed or choppy speech will settle.

4 of 171 posts.

Stuttering, mood-specific lisping or separated syllables prompt questions about waiting or therapy.

- "Like 5 days ago she started with this little stutter. Any word that ends in a T, D, as well as some other letters, has become T-T-T or D-D-D." [ar022-0066]
- "My toddler 2.3y has been speaking in 3-4 word sentences for around 6 months now, but it still sounds very choppy and like he’s new to speaking?" [ar006-0120]

**But:** An assessment was reassuring, although fluency had not improved. "We had him evaluated by E.I. and they stopped the evaluation about an hour in and said that they didn’t need to continue because he was well past the “passing grade” and would not get services and that his speech pattern and fluency was still in the realm of normal and would even out the more he talks and as he gets older, but that was 2-3 months ago and despite him adding more and more words to his vocabulary, the speech fluency hasn’t improved." [ar006-0123]

### The parent fears a new baby has made the toddler’s behaviour worse.

4 of 171 posts.

Parents describe regression or anticipate it before birth, and ask how to manage both children.

- "My 2 1/2 year old (3 in July) has been having SEVERE behavior issues since our 3rd baby was born a few weeks ago… but he also struggled with this behavior a bit before baby came too, and I’m wondering if it’s worth an evaluation." [ar033-0001]
- "I just can’t help but think there will be some regression/attention seeking behavior once this new baby does arrive. They will also be starting preschool in September. This will be a lot of changes and I’m nervous. Any common tips and tricks to ensure a smooth transition?" [ar046-0052]

**But:** Affection for the baby can remain. "She is affectionate towards her little sister, wants to hold her and snuggle all the time." [ar014-0029]

### The parent wants independence without losing the child or trusting strangers.

3 of 171 posts.

Running away, escaping supervision and approaching unfamiliar adults make ordinary outings difficult.

- "How do I keep her safe while also encouraging independence? So far, I keep her in the pram in busy and unfamiliar areas. She hates the pram and wants to walk. I totally understand but it's not always safe and she tends to drop my hand and run." [ar070-0044]
- "The other day my dad was supposed to watch her while I was working from home. I thought she was with him but she went to the bathroom. We had some guys working at the house and apparently she went to them after the bathroom with her pants down to show them the toilet paper with poop. It concerned me so much that my dad didn’t notice this and neither did I." [ar028-0161]

**But:** Most encounters are uneventful. "Most people just smile and walk on, but some tend to stop and start chatting to her or encourage her to give them a hug (i dont want her hugging strangers)." [ar070-0045]

### Other: The parent wonders how a toddler remembered nursing.

1 of 171 posts.

A two-year-old nurses after stopping at seven months; the parent asks about memory, instinct or comfort.

- "But she immediately latched and started nursing like she remembered exactly what to do. 😅 She stayed on one breast for a while and then switched to the other one by herself." [ar043-0109]
- "Has this happened to anyone else? Can toddlers actually remember how to nurse after such a long time, or was it more of an instinct/comfort thing?" [ar043-0111]

**But:** nothing found against it.

## Near posts

### The parent asks whether disrupted sleep reflects a developmental change.

12 of 23 posts.

Lost independence, repeated requests, night crying and changing naps prompt reassurance, schedule changes and possible medical checks.

- "She's either really cranky because she doesn't get a nap or she struggles to fall asleep at night because she naps so close to bed time." [ar075-0186]
- "Every night we put him to bed, and he’ll chill for a bit but then get up to come see us. Can be once or sometimes up to 6+ times. Various reasons (I want socks, I want a hug, I can’t find X plushy, or just not wanting to sleep)." [ar034-0130]

**But:** Difficulty can depend on the setting. "" [ar038-0143]

### The parent asks whether physical signs reflect ordinary growth.

6 of 23 posts.

Screening results, appetite, leg pain, clothing proportions, teething and constipation prompt questions; some ages are unknown.

- "I don't know if it's a growth spurt or what but lately my 3 year old has a ravenous appetite. On top of her 3 usual meals she's now wanting 3-4 snacks a day." [ar073-0119]
- "I assumed it was growing pains, but it's been going on quite a while and it's only ever the left side. I remember my legs hurting a lot when I was young, usually the left side as well, but never enough to make me cry like that. It did last for years though, not months." [ar047-0074]

**But:** Failed screening followed no earlier sensory concerns. "" [ar049-0001]

### The parent asks when practical skills should develop and how to help.

2 of 23 posts.

One age is unknown; another child is a middle-schooler struggling with mathematics, beyond this niche.

- "At what age could your child do these “childhood” skills?" [ar056-0010]
- "Do we get him a tutor? Do we find fun games? Anyone experienced something similar? TIA" [ar058-0093]

**But:** The older child otherwise enjoyed school that day. "When I picked him up he said he had a great day and told me his schedule and he got to catch up with friends." [ar058-0090]

### The parent wants investigation after prolonged distress despite advanced speech.

1 of 23 posts.

Current age is unknown; the mother describes sensory needs and seeks blood work and an autism evaluation.

- "My middle daughter has been miserable since she before she turned 2. At first, I chalked it up to terrible twos. She’s extremely advanced in language so it was never an inability to communicate." [ar059-0135]
- "I’ve realized she’s a very intense sensory seeker, constantly running and throwing her body on to things. The main way we get her to fall asleep now is by burrito rolling her in a blanket, really basically swaddling her." [ar059-0138]

**But:** Some active settings suit the child. "She does extremely well in other high sensory input situations, toddler times and playground she thrives without tears I think due to the ability to run and jump and throw herself around." [ar059-0138]

### The writer asks which subtle changes made parents suspect a problem.

1 of 23 posts.

No child’s age or personal case is given; examples concern withdrawal, interests, sleep, eating and self-description.

- "What was the thing that made you stop and think "something's not right"?" [ar036-0077]
- "From what I've seen its almost never one big moment. Its a kid who used to talk the whole car ride home and now just puts headphones in. Or they quit something they loved and say they "got bored of it." And when you ask, they say theyre fine." [ar036-0079]

**But:** nothing found against it.

### The parent asks whether daily routines feel like war for others.

1 of 23 posts.

The stated range is three to five; no individual child’s age or attempted response is given.

- "Serious question. Is dinner, brushing teeth, and bedtime war in every household with toddlers or is that not the norm??" [ar075-0193]
- "Like how do parents create an environment that allows for that process to be smooth and calm." [ar075-0193]

**But:** nothing found against it.

## Across the on-niche posts

### The parent has tried changing responses, practising skills or pursuing care.

127 of 171 posts.

Attempts include consequences, routines, exposure, activities, language correction and medical testing.

- "Otherwise, discipline is usually a consequence for his actions that he can understand, like taking the toy when he throws it and telling him he can have it back when he can be gentle. We've demonstrated and have also had him demonstrate gentle behavior in these instances, and then he gets the toy back." [ar066-0032]
- "Long story short: our daughter’s 12 month bloodwork showed low hemoglobin (9.7). No one told or flagged us (the results weren’t even available for us to view in the portal). We later learned of the reading at 21 months and insisted on a fresh blood draw. It showed hemoglobin of 8.6. So we insisted on further tests and it showed ferritin of 6. The doctor didn’t seem overly concerned but at least now is treating it with iron supplements." [ar078-0024]

**But:** Trying does not guarantee relief. "We do try to give her a warning and then the tears just start at that time instead of when the noise actually starts." [ar006-0111]

### The parent hears conflicting judgments and advice from other adults.

94 of 171 posts.

Relatives, teachers and clinicians offer warnings, referrals, support or reassurance.

- "That went as good as you could imagine 🤣 my MIL told me i needed to medicate her or spank her. Neither of which I’m doing. She told me “my kids didn’t act this way!” Yeah, because they were probably scared of you…" [ar032-0017]
- "The teacher said that she has trouble focusing sometimes. I asked if the focus issue was concerning (as in, do I need to get her evaluated for an attention deficit disorder) and the teacher said not yet, but that there is a real possibility that my daughter could fall behind as the school year moves along because she is not applying herself." [ar006-0261]

**But:** Some advice is reassuring. "Her pediatrician reassured us that it's a phase and advised us to keep exposing her to social situations without too much pressure, which we've been doing. I just hate seeing her struggle." [ar028-0186]

### The parent reports buying practical items for everyday activities.

3 of 171 posts.

Three posts explicitly name purchases: ear defenders and train tickets in one, special markers in another, and Vinted clothes in the third; prices are unknown.

- "My daughter (2 years, 3 months) absolutely freaks out over any sort of loud noise. The lawnmower, vacuum, hair dryer, etc. Even if we’re in the house and my husband is mowing the lawn she loses her mind. I bought her the sound proof ear muffs but she refuses to wear them." [ar006-0108]
- "I buy some markers that only work on a specific type of paper." [ar015-0077]
- "Which is frustrating when I but clothes from Vinted and don't realise there is a small button at the back (I am now more careful)." [ar026-0036]

**But:** Buying does not ensure the activity will work. "I bought my family tickets for a “Donuts with dad” for a Father’s Day gift on our local train and then it hit me after I bought them that it probably wasn’t a good idea because she’s going to freak out the second the train blows its whistle." [ar006-0109]

### The parent feels guilty, overwhelmed or helpless about the child’s difficulties.

104 of 171 posts.

Parents also describe embarrassment, anger, anxiety and exhaustion.

- "Feel like I’m going completely insane with guilt over this and so upset I didn’t notice this faster." [ar051-0037]
- "I'm so overwhelmed and heartbroken. I feel awful for even thinking this, but a small part of me is almost regretting having a second. Not only do I feel like it has it caused all of this turmoil with my toddler, but I'm terrified of going through this stage a second time." [ar014-0031]

**But:** Peer reassurance can bring relief. "***EDIT:*** *Thanks to everyone for your input, I'm feeling way better now, actually. I know "your 3 year old will be hell and you'd best get used to it" doesn't sound overly reassuring, but it is. I'm glad he's normal, even if my mother swears blind that he's not* 🤣" [ar038-0060]

